EVERY HALFWORD IN ONE COMPLETE THUMB DECODE REGION.

Status: closed-decode-subtree specification for sixteen-bit ARMv7-A T32,
limited exactly to halfwords 0xBA00 through 0xBAFF, inclusive.
It is a finite part of the ARMv7-A instruction encoding space,
not a claim of completeness for the entire Thumb instruction set.
The source language's permitted operations are numbers 65, 66 and 67
in "All and only these initial operations are permitted.english".

PINNED NORMATIVE REFERENCE.

ARM DDI 0406C.d, ID040418, Chapter A6,
section A6.2.5 "Miscellaneous 16-bit instructions",
Table A6-6; the "Byte-Reverse Word",
"Byte-Reverse Packed Halfword", and
"Byte-Reverse Signed Halfword" entries, with
"other encodings in this space are UNDEFINED."
For complete semantics consult ARM DDI 0406C.d,
REV at A8-563, REV16 at A8-565 and
REVSH at A8-567, and any special instruction
restrictions on execution in an IT block.

A secondary readable reference is
ARM Compiler armasm Reference Guide, ARM DUI 0802B,
Section 3.70 (printed pages 3-125 to 3-126).
This secondary reference is not permission to import
non-ARMv7-A instructions.

AUTHORITATIVE IN-REPOSITORY ENUMERATION.

The data-only source file
"_/fixtures/armv7-a-t32-byte-reversal-halfwords.tsv"
at repository root contains exactly 256 DATA ROWS,
one for each distinct hexadecimal halfword
0xBA00 through 0xBAFF, with fields declaring
its decode class, permitted long-English
operation number, encoded source/destination
registers if any, and admission status.

This document describes its generator-independent
rules so that the TSV is independently auditable
instead of being accepted just because it has
256 lines. Neither file is an executable decoder.

PART ONE. WHAT THE 256 HALFWORDS HAVE IN COMMON.

All these encodings have the most significant
eight instruction bits equal to binary 10111010,
which is hexadecimal BA. The lowest eight bits
range from hexadecimal 00 to hexadecimal FF.
Each bit pattern is a SINGLE TWO-BYTE instruction
halfword in Thumb state, not the first halfword of
a four-byte Thumb instruction.

The two high bits of the varying low byte,
bits seven and six of the entire halfword,
select an encoding subgroup.

The remaining six low bits are divided into:
  three bits, positions five, four, three,
  encoding the number of a possible source
  low register, from R0 through R7;
  three bits, positions two, one, zero,
  encoding the number of a possible destination
  low register, from R0 through R7.

For valid REV, REV16, and REVSH instructions,
the source is therefore:
    (encoded halfword >> 3) AND binary 111.
For those valid instructions, the destination is:
    encoded halfword AND binary 111.

That bit extraction is meaningful as register
selection only after a valid operation class
has been established. In the reserved subgroup
it is incorrect to claim a successfully
decoded REV-type operation with those fields.

PART TWO. THE FOUR AND ONLY FOUR SUBGROUPS.

SUBGROUP ONE:
When bits 7:6 equal binary 00,
the complete halfword is in the inclusive
range 0xBA00 through 0xBA3F.
Every one of the 64 patterns in this range
is the T1 encoding for REV applied to the
encoded low source and destination registers.
These are conditionally admitted to English
Major C as operation 65, provided the
machine state and memory execution
permissions are otherwise valid.

Examples:
    0xBA00 selects REV R0,R0.
    0xBA0A selects REV R2,R1.
    0xBA3F selects REV R7,R7.

SUBGROUP TWO:
When bits 7:6 equal binary 01,
the complete halfword is in the inclusive
range 0xBA40 through 0xBA7F.
Every one of the 64 patterns in this range
is a T1 REV16 operating on low registers.
They are admitted as long operation 66,
subject to otherwise valid execution context.

Examples:
    0xBA40 selects REV16 R0,R0.
    0xBA63 selects REV16 R3,R4.
    0xBA7F selects REV16 R7,R7.

SUBGROUP THREE:
When bits 7:6 equal binary 10,
the complete halfword is in the inclusive
range 0xBA80 through 0xBABF.
The ARMv7-A T32 miscellaneous decode table
does NOT allocate these 64 patterns to
a valid data-processing operation.
This subtree class is UNDEFINED and
English Major C SHALL REJECT all 64
machine encoding interpretations here.

Those six lowest bits are still bits,
but the current operation grant may not
reinterpret them as source and destination
registers for a made-up data instruction.

Examples:
    0xBA80 is NOT REV.
    0xBAA8 is NOT RBIT.
    0xBABF is NOT REVSH.
The fact that there is a separate
32-bit native RBIT operation does not
fill the unused 16-bit subgroup.

SUBGROUP FOUR:
When bits 7:6 equal binary 11,
the complete halfword is in the inclusive
range 0xBAC0 through 0xBAFF.
Every one of the 64 patterns in this range
is the T1 REVSH, reversing bytes in the
low halfword and sign-extending the result.
This is long English operation 67.

Examples:
    0xBAC0 selects REVSH R0,R0.
    0xBAF5 selects REVSH R5,R6.
    0xBAFF selects REVSH R7,R7.

PART THREE. THIS IS A PARTITION, NOT FOUR GUESSES.

The four closed hexadecimal intervals are
pairwise disjoint. Each contains exactly
64 distinct numerical halfwords, because
the six low register fields carry exactly
2^6 combinations.

Their union is all of hexadecimal BA00..BAFF,
which contains 256 halfwords.
Therefore the partition has no gaps and
no overlapping assignments.

The class counts are:
  REV   : 64 permitted candidate encodings;
  REV16 : 64 permitted candidate encodings;
  UNDEFINED: 64 encoding patterns that MUST be rejected;
  REVSH : 64 permitted candidate encodings.

"Candidate" means the encoding is allocated
in the architecture and has a corresponding
permission in the language. It does NOT assert
successful execution without the necessary
Thumb state, decoding context, ITSTATE validity,
executable mapping, exception rules or
target-specific premises.

PART FOUR. AN INDEPENDENT DIAGNOSTIC CHECK OF THE SUBTREE.

A diagnostic source with ALL 256 .hword
values in strictly increasing order was
assembled in a temporary analysis environment
using clang 17 with target armv7a-none-eabi
and Thumb instruction state, then decoded
with LLVM objdump 17 under thumbv7-none-eabi.

The inspection yielded exactly:
    64 REV results;
    64 REV16 results;
    64 unknown results;
    64 REVSH results.

In particular, the boundary values
BA00, BA3F, BA40, BA7F,
BA80, BABF, BAC0, BAFF
were cross-checked individually, and the
general 256-row output matched the
four-region formula with ZERO discrepancies.

Those are diagnostic assembler/disassembler
observations, NOT a compiled English Major C
binary, independent AICI behavioral
acceptance, actual target ARM execution,
or proof that LLVM has no decoding bugs.
ARM DDI 0406C.d remains the normative
source for legal encodings.

The diagnostic generated no maintained
consumer build stage. The project may not
claim the foreign diagnostic compiler is
its qualified ICK or Android NDK backend.

PART FIVE. WHAT A FUTURE READER MUST REJECT.

An implemented English Major C decoder
must fail on the following source mutations:

Case A: classify BA80 as REV, REV16 or REVSH.
This contradicts the ARM decode table.

Case B: classify BAAA as RBIT merely
because REVSH and RBIT both reverse data.
The selected 16-bit subtree does not
contain RBIT.

Case C: swap the three source-register
bits with the three destination-register
bits, for example interpreting BA0A
as REV R1,R2 rather than REV R2,R1.

Case D: treat BA0A as a 32-bit instruction,
or consume the immediately following
halfword as its second half.
This is a two-byte instruction.

Case E: change Z or C when a valid
REV/REV16/REVSH executes normally.
These operations preserve N/Z/C/V.

Case F: permit R8, SP, LR, or PC as
the source or destination of one of
these T1 halfwords. The bit field
physically encodes only three bits.

Case G: treat a halfword taken from a
normal DATA load as though that load
already executed the corresponding
REV instruction. Instruction fetch
and data access are distinct.

Case H: claim that all of T32 has
been exhaustively audited merely
because all 256 BAxx halfwords
have been accounted for.

Case I: interpret BA80 as harmless NOP.
The unused subtree is unallocated
and must not be silently executed as
a successful permitted operation.

Case J: reverse the memory byte order
of a loaded object simply because
an internal register undergoes REV.
That is a different memory
operation and representation choice.

PART SIX. WHY THIS PARTICULAR SUBTREE WAS CHOSEN.

The user's language-design premise requires
"all and only the following operations"
to have a meaning that can be independently
checked rather than approximated by mnemonic
lists or prose labels.

A short list of representative REV
encodings alone would not meet that test.
This subtree permits exact accounting
over a full finite set of instruction
bits, including an unallocated class.

It also demonstrates a central distinction
in the English Major C design: bits
representing source/destination operands
are DATA ABOUT the instruction encoding,
while decoding and execution are separate
steps with their own permissions and effects.

The same method can later be extended
to additional subtrees of A6.2 and A6.3,
including condition branches, immediate
arithmetic, load/store forms, and
32-bit data-processing instructions.

The full Thumb decoder is not yet
specified or implemented, and the project
shall not infer that the uninspected
regions are complete.

END OF THE 256-HALFWORD DECODE CONTRACT.
